We manipulate these equations to get $p$ or $q$ in terms of the other, and other square expressions by strategy. We square both of the two equation
\begin{align*}
p^2 + 2pq + q^2 & = p^2 q^2, \\
p^2 - 14p + q^2 & = 0. \\
\end{align*}
We multiply the first equation, $p^2 q^2,$ by 4
\begin{align*}
4p^2 q^2 & = 4p^2 + 8pq + 4q^2, \\
4p^2 - 56p + 4q^2 & = -8pq.\\
\end{align*}
Now, given that we have the equation $p^2 - 14p + q^2 = 0,$ it's convenient to manipulate this equation
\begin{align*}
- 56p & = - 8pq + (p^2 - 14p + q^2), \\
0 & = - 8pq + p^2 + q^2, \\
p^2 + 8pq + q^2 & = 0 \\
(p + q)^2 & = 0
\end{align*}
Thus, $pq = -7.$ Hence, $(p,q)$ = $(7 + \sqrt{91},7 - \sqrt{91}).  $(q,p)$ does not work here, because we're given $pq = -7,$ and if $$(q,p) = (7 +\sqrt{91},7 - \sqrt{91},)$$ 
Then this wouldn't be possible.  So then, $$\frac{1}{\frac{1}{p^2}+\frac{1}{q^2}}  = \frac{pq}{p^2 + q^2}.$$ We can find the value of the denominator by distributing it
\begin{align*}
p^2 + q^2 & = (7 + \sqrt{91})^2 + (7 - \sqrt{91})^2, \\
& = \left( 49 + 2 \sqrt{91} \cdot 7 \right) + \left( 49 - 2 \sqrt{91} \cdot 7 \right), \\
& = \left( 49 + 98 \sqrt{91}  \right) + \left( 49 - 98 \sqrt{91} \right) \\
& = \left( 98 \right).
\end{align*}
This means that, $$\frac{1}{\frac{1}{p^2}+\frac{1}{q^2}} = \frac{pq}{98}.$$ Since $(7 + \sqrt{91})(7 - \sqrt{91}) =  - 7^2 = -7,$ we can conclude that $$\frac{1}{\frac{1}{p^2}+\frac{1}{q^2}} = \frac{-7}{98}.$$ We can simplify this
\begin{align*}
\frac{-7}{98}& = -\frac{1}{14}.
\end{align*}
