\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} 3x+4y-12z&=10 \\ -2x-3y+9z&=-4 \end{align*}
We want to find the value of $x$. We can eliminate $y$ by multiplying the first equation by 3 and the second equation by 4, then adding the resulting equations.
\begin{align*} 3(3x+4y-12z) &= 3(10) \\ 4(-2x-3y+9z) &= 4(-4) \end{align*}
This gives us:
\begin{align*} 9x+12y-36z &= 30 \\ -8x-12y+36z &= -16 \end{align*}
Adding the two equations, we get:
\begin{align*} (9x-8x) + (12y-12y) + (-36z+36z) &= 30-16 \\ x &= 14 \end{align*}
Thus, $x=14$.

Now we verify the solution.
Substituting $x=14$ into the given equations:
\begin{align*} 3(14)+4y-12z&=10 \\ -2(14)-3y+9z&=-4 \end{align*}
\begin{align*} 42+4y-12z&=10 \\ -28-3y+9z&=-4 \end{align*}
\begin{align*} 4y-12z&=-32 \\ -3y+9z&=24 \end{align*}
Dividing the first equation by 4 and the second equation by 3, we get:
\begin{align*} y-3z&=-8 \\ -y+3z&=8 \end{align*}
Adding the two equations, we get $0=0$, which means there are infinitely many solutions.
Let $z=t$. Then $y=3t-8$.
Substituting $x=14$, $y=3t-8$, and $z=t$ into the first equation:
