To find $A-B$, we multiply through by $(x-1)(x+1)$ to eliminate the fractions: \begin{align*}
\frac A{x-1}\cdot (x-1)(x+1) &\qquad+\qquad\frac B{x+1}\cdot (x-1)(x+1)= \frac{x+2}{x^2-1}\cdot (x-1)(x+1),\\
\Rightarrow\qquad A(x+1)&\qquad+B(x-1)=\frac{(x+2)}{(x-1)(x+1)}\cdot (x-1)(x+1),\\
\Rightarrow\qquad A(x+1)+B(x-1)&=x+2,\\
\Rightarrow\qquad A(x+1)+B(x-1)-x-2&=0.
\end{align*}To solve for $A$, let $x=1$: \begin{align*}
A(1+1)-B(1-1)-1-2&=0\\
\Rightarrow\qquad2A-1-2&=0\\
\Rightarrow\qquad2A&=1+2=3\\
\Rightarrow\qquad A&=\frac{3}{2}
\end{align*}To solve for $B$, let $x=-1$: \begin{align*}
A(-1+1)+B(-1-1)+1+2&=0\\
\Rightarrow\qquad B(-2)+3&=0\\
\Rightarrow\qquad-2B&=-3\\
\Rightarrow\qquad B&=\frac{3}{2}
\end{align*}
Finally, we can use the formula $A-B$ to compute: \begin{align*}
A-B &= \frac{3}{2}-\frac{3}{2}\\
&=0
\end{align*}
\boxed{0}

