To find the different three-digit odd numbers, we need to have a units digit of 3 or 5. Since we cannot repeat any digit, we have 1 choice for the units place. Now, we need to choose two digits for the tens and hundreds place from the remaining 3 digits, 4, 5 and 6, without repeating any digits. For the tens place, we have 3 choices, as any of the 3 remaining digits can be chosen for the tens place. 
Once, we have chosen the digit for the tens place, for the hundreds place, we will have only 2 choices left, as one digit has already been used for the tens place.

The number of possible combinations will thus be:

\begin{align*}
3 \text{ (for units place)} \cdot 3 \text{ (for tens place)} \cdot 2 \text{ (for hundreds place)} = 18.
\end{align*}

\boxed{18}

