Since $\triangle{ABC}$ is a right triangle, we have $\cos{B}=\frac{\text{adjacent}}{\text{hypotenuse}}=\frac{AC}{AB}=\frac{3}{5}.$
From the Pythagorean Theorem, we know $AC^2+BC^2=AB^2$.  We find that 
\begin{align*}
AC^2+BC^2&=AB^2\\
\Rightarrow\qquad AC^2+(BC)^2&=(9)^2\\
\Rightarrow\qquad AC^2&=(81)-BC^2,
\end{align*} 
with $AC=\frac{3}{5}(AB)=\frac{27}{5}$ and $BC=\frac{BC}{1}=\frac{6}{5}$. Putting it into the equation we have \begin{align*}
\left(\frac{27}{5}\right)^2&=(81)-BC^2\\
\Rightarrow\qquad \frac{729}{25}&=81-BC^2\\
\Rightarrow\qquad BC&=\sqrt{81-\frac{729}{25}}\\
\Rightarrow\qquad BC&=\sqrt{\frac{2025-729}{25}}\\
\Rightarrow\qquad BC&=\sqrt{\frac{1296}{25}}\\
\Rightarrow\qquad BC&=\frac{36}{5}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, by definition of the cosine of an angle within a right triangle, \begin{align*}
\cos{C}=\frac{AC}{AB}=\frac{27/5}{\frac{36}{5}}=\frac{27/5}{36/5}=\boxed{\frac{3}{4}}.
\end{align*}

Since $315^\circ = 360^\circ - 45^\circ,$ and cosine is periodic with period $360^\circ,$ we can write
\begin{align*}
\cos 315^\circ &= \cos(360^\circ - 45^\circ)\\
&= \cos(-45^\circ)\\
&= \cos 45^\circ\\
&= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}.
\end{align*}
\boxed{\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}}

