Since the $2^{nd}$, $4^{th}$, $6^{th}$, and so on terms have coefficients of $3$ and the rest of the terms have coefficients of $2$, this polynomial is equivalent to
\begin{align*}
\left(2x^{1999} + 2x^{1997} + 2x^{1995} + \dots + 2x + 2\right)x^2 + 3x^{2000} + 3x^{1998} + 3x^{1996} + \dots + 3x.
\end{align*}
The $x^2$ term in $x^{2000}$ coefficients corresponds to an x value of zero i.e. when x=0 
Therefore the expression reduces to 
\begin{align*}
\left(3 + 3x^{-2} + 3x^{-4} + \dots\right)x^2 + 2x^2 + 2x^{1995} + \dots + 2x.
\end{align*}
Now the sum 
\begin{align*}
S &= 3 + 3x^{-2} + 3x^{-4} + \dots
\end{align*} 
is a geometric seires with first term $a_1=3$ and co-efficient ratio $r=x^{-2}$. Therefore, for  $|x|>1$, this sum is given as follows 
\begin{align*}
S &=  \frac{3(1 - (x^{-2})^{n})}{1 - x^{-2}} = \frac{3(1 - x^{-2n})}{1 - x^{-2}}.
\end{align*}
As we have that $x^{2001}$ term order, this is thus a valid expression for this polynomial. 
Dividing this polynomial by $x^2$ gives a polynomial of degree $1999$ with leading term $2x^{1997}$ and all the other positive-degree coefficients $>0$; its graph therefore has the same sign as the leading term, $-2x^{1997}$. 
Thus, when $x > 1$, the sign of the polynomial is $+$. However, the polynomial has the value $3\cdot x^2$ when $x \to \pm \infty$, so the polynomial ultimately has a sign of $-$ in each case. The interval $(1,\infty)$ therefore contains exactly one zero, say $t_1$. 
The other zero is $t_{1999}$ such that 
$$2x^{1997}t_{1999} +  \dots +(t_{1999})^{2}+t_{1999}+3=0$$ where $\dots$ represents terms whose total degree in $t_{1999}$ is higher than $-1$, so that $(-t_{1999})^{1999}$ is the leading term. We see that
\begin{align*}
-t_{1999}
\end{align*}
has the same sign as $(-t_{1999})^{1999}$, so that the sign of $(-t_{1999})^{1999}$ is the same as that of the polynomial.
But we know from above that the polynomial has a sign of $-$, so we must have \begin{align*}
-t_{1999} < 0,
\end{align*}or $t_{1999} > 0$. The conditions $t_{1999}\in\mathbb{R}$, $t_{1999} > 0$, and $$2x^{1997}t_{1999} +  \dots +(t_{1999})^{2}+t_{1999}+3=0$$determine the root $t_{1999}$ uniquely, so the polynomial has exactly one real root.

