Define $t=f(1).$ For any positive real number $x,$ let $y=1.$ Therefore,
\begin{align*}
f(x)&=f(x\cdot1)=f(x)f(1)-2\\
\Rightarrow \qquad f(x)&=f(x)t-2\\
\Rightarrow \qquad f(x)+2&=f(x)t
\end{align*}Since this equation is true for all positive real numbers $x,$ let $x$ take on any such values. This shows that $t$ is a factor in every output of $f(x).$ 

First, suppose $t \neq 1.$ Thus $t$ is a unique factor, and since $f(x)$ takes positive real numbers into real numbers, we can see that the value of $f(x)$ must be unique up to the value of $x,$ and that it must not have any leading coefficient because of how  $t$ is the factor due to our function equation. We write $f(x)$ in the form $ax$ for some constant $a.$ Hence,
\begin{align*}
f(x)&=ax,\\
f(xy)&=f(x)f(y)=axy.
\end{align*}
Substituting into the given equation and simplifying, we have
\begin{align*}
f(xy)&=f(x)f(y)-2\\
\Rightarrow \qquad axy=a x a y -2 &\quad(\text{substituting values in the given equation 1})\\
\Rightarrow \qquad axy=a^2 xy - 2 &\quad(\text{splitting the product in terms of its factors})\\
\Rightarrow \qquad a^2- a &\quad(\text{simplifying as }xy \neq 0)\\
\Rightarrow \qquad  a^2-a+2&=0.
\end{align*}
The left-hand side cannot be solved by factoring, and the discriminant $\Delta=a^2-4<0.$ We can use the quadratic equation formula and find,
\begin{align*}
a &=\frac{-b \pm \sqrt{\Delta}}{2a}\\
