To find the day of the week, we use modular arithmetic and the concept of remainders when dividing by $7$. 

January 1, 2000 was a Saturday, which we can take as the reference point $R$. Let $b$ represent the number of days remaining in the year, $c$ the number of days between years, $d$ the remainder of years from a reference point, and $N$ the desired day of the week.

The day of the week $n$ years after our starting reference point $R$ is given by the formula \begin{align*} 
n = (n+d) \mod 7 
\end{align*}, where the parentheses are used to discount the remainders occurring within a prior year. This optimizes the equation, ensuring that a remainder of one year will prevent a previous year's remainder from being counted.

Every year that is divisible by $4$ will have an extra day, other than in years that are divisible by $100$. However, if years divisible by $400$ should necessarily have an extra day.

The formula to minimize calendar misfits (counting weeks) between reference point $T_0$ and desired year $T_n$ is given by:
\begin{align*}
n &= \left( \left\lfloor \frac{T_n}{4} \right\rfloor - \left\lfloor \frac{T_n}{100} \right\rfloor - \left\lfloor \frac{T_n}{400} \right\rfloor \right) \mod 7
\end{align*}

assuming $T_0=2000$. Plugging into the formula the desired date: $T_n=1960$, the information can be shown to be 
\begin{align*}
n &= \left( \left\lfloor \frac{1960}{4} \right\rfloor - \left\lfloor \frac{1960}{100} \right\rfloor - \left\lfloor \frac{1960}{400} \right\rfloor \right) \mod 7 \\
&= \left( \left\lfloor 490 \right\rfloor - \left\lfloor 19.6 \right\rfloor - \left\lfloor 4.9 \right\rfloor \right) \mod 7\\
&= \left( \left\lfloor 490 \right\rfloor - \left\lfloor 19.6 \right\rfloor - \left\lfloor 4.9 \right\rfloor \right) \mod 7\\
&= (490 - 19 - 4) \mod 7 \\
&= 467\ \mod 7\\
&= \boxed{5}
\end{align*} 
January 1, 1960 was a Thursday.

