\end{align*}
However, we're dividing by $10$ and the modulus $20$ doesn't contain any factor of $10$,
we can leave $a + b$ in the fraction just for the expression in the congruence 
\begin{align*}
10(a+b) &\equiv -17\pmod{20}.
\end{align*}
We want to find an integer $k$ such that
\begin{align*}
-17 +20k & \equiv 0\pmod{20}\\
\begin{matrix}
-17  \\
-17 +20 \\
