Since we are looking for $n$ in the modular equation $31n\equiv 3\pmod{2293}$, it might be helpful to look at the modular equation $31n \equiv 31\cdot 37\equiv 1147 \equiv 3\pmod{2293}$. Thus our equation can be simplified to $0\equiv 1146 \pmod{2293}$. Additionally since $\gcd(31,2293) = 1$, the modular inverse exists, that is the existence of $m$ such that \begin{align*} 
31m &\equiv 1\pmod{2293} \\
\end{align*}
Hence we can write 
\begin{align*} 
3m &\equiv 1146 \pmod{2293}  \\
31m &\equiv 3 \pmod{2293}  \\
(31\cdot  m)\pmod{2293} &\equiv 3 \pmod{2293}  
\end{align*}  and we now find that the solution is simply $n = 31m$.
We now must find $m$ and must want the modular inverse of $31$ in mod $2293$, to find this value easily we can utilize the Extended Euclidean algorithm that is as follows.
http://mathworld.wolfram.com/EuclideanAlgorithm.html
Thus  \begin{align*}
2293 & = 74\cdot 31 + 9, \\
31  & = 3\cdot 9 + 4, \\
9   & = 2\cdot 4 + 1 \\
\end{align*} 
We can now write $1$ as a linear combination of $31$ and $2293$ as follows, using the reverse order of congruences. Hence we have 
\begin{align*}
1 & =  9 - 2\cdot 4\\
& \equiv 9-2(31-3\cdot9)\\
& \equiv 11\cdot 9\\
& \equiv 11(2293-74\cdot31)\\
& \equiv  11(2293)-11\cdot74\cdot 31\\
\end{align*}
Hence we have \begin{align*}
31 \cdot n & \equiv 1 \pmod{2293} \\
31\cdot (-11\cdot 74\cdot 31)& \equiv  1 \pmod{2293} \\
n & =  -11\cdot 74\cdot 31 \ pmod{2293}\\
n & = \boxed{-24794}
\end{align*}
